   "source": [
    "# Quiz: Working with Data: Relational Databases\n",
    "\n",
    "> **On Alps:** These are the questions of this lesson's pre-lecture and post-lecture quizzes, from the course's quiz app, with the answers the quiz app accepts."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Pre-lecture quiz"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"dsb-quiz-05-pre-1\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>A database can be considered a table with columns and rows</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>True</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. A relational database is built on tables, and each table has columns and rows.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>False</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. A relational database is built on tables of columns and rows.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"dsb-quiz-05-pre-2\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>Most databases are comprised of</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>one table</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. One table can repeat the same data, like Tokyo's name, over and over. Databases split data across many tables.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>one spreadsheet</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. A database is like a spreadsheet, but it spreads data across multiple tables.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>many tables</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. Relational databases divide information between multiple tables.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"dsb-quiz-05-pre-3\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>You can avoid duplicating column names by</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>creating many tables</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Extra tables are not enough on their own. They need relationships, like ids that link them.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>creating tables with relationships built in</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. Splitting data into tables linked by relationships avoids duplication.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>sticking to one big table</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. One big table can repeat the same data, like Tokyo's name and country, over and over.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Post-lecture quiz"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"dsb-quiz-05-post-1\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>A primary key is</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>a value used to identify one specific row in a table</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. A primary key is a value that identifies one specific row in a table.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>a value used to make values unique</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. A primary key's job is to identify one specific row in a table.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>a value used to force capitalization</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. A primary key is not about capitalization. It identifies one specific row in a table.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"dsb-quiz-05-post-2\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>A numeric 'ID' column would be a good primary key</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>true</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. A primary key should almost always be a number or other id. Often it is a number made automatically.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>false</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. A primary key should almost always be a number or other id that never changes.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"dsb-quiz-05-post-3\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>A foreign key is used to</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>values that reference the ids in a separate table</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. A foreign key holds values that point to the ids in another table, like city_id in the rainfall table.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>values that reference the strings in a separate table</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. A foreign key references ids, which are primary keys from another table.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>preserve values that change over time</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. A foreign key is a reference, or pointer, to the id of a row in another table.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  }
 ],
